# Target C-II — `g=0.0076` forces a live linear dominant double-outer family through `t<=1.860`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only structural consequence  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with the corrected `g=0.0076` matched atomic floor  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The earlier dominant double-outer theorem forced more than `0.3025D` dominant-orbit O--O trains only in the window `t<=1.843`.  The scalar floor has since moved above that window, making the old statement vacuous for current survivors.

Using the corrected matched atomic floor

\[
C_{76}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.0076,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

the same bootstrap remains effective on the much wider live window

\[
\boxed{t={q-k\over D}\le1.860.}
\]

After freezing shrinking windows around the dominant exact `pi/3` train orbit, let `H_D` be the number of dominant-window complete stopped trains whose two terminal face incidences are both selected outer occurrences in the joint-retention construction.  Then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H_D\over D}>0.0740.}
\tag{D76.1}

Thus every current survivor in

\[
1.84332\lesssim t\le1.860
\]

contains a linear family of complete trains from one exact three-phase orbit that run from the selected outer walk back to the selected outer walk.

---

## 1. Inputs

Write

\[
t={q-k\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D},
\qquad
\delta={d_{out}\over D},
\]

\[
U={9t\over2}-2\pi-2\delta-\kappa/2.
\tag{D76.2}

The corrected matched atomic theorem gives

\[
\boxed{U\ge C_{76}-o(1).}
\tag{D76.3}

Put

\[
m_2(U)={U+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-U^2}\over2},
\qquad
r(U)=U-m_2(U).
\tag{D76.4}

The hostile-audited shared-retention and complement-rhombus-incidence stack gives retained dominant outer-occurrence mass `z` and retained complement O--O pair mass `p` with

\[
\boxed{
z+p\ge
S(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1),}
\tag{D76.5}

\[
S(t,\delta,\kappa)
=\pi-U-{t+\kappa\over2}+m_2(U),
\tag{D76.6}

and

\[
\boxed{
p\le
{\delta-F(z)+r(U)^2\over2(1-\delta)}+o(1),}
\tag{D76.7}

where

\[
F(z)=z-6\sin(z/6).
\tag{D76.8}

The retained dominant selected edges themselves are disjoint outer-edge occurrences, so

\[
\boxed{
\delta\ge F(z)-o(1).}
\tag{D76.9}

No feasible-triangle derivative estimate from the scalar-floor theorem is needed below.  In particular, the `delta<0.05` bound used there is not an input to this structural bootstrap.

---

## 2. Uniform envelopes through `t<=1.860`

Fix

\[
T_0=1.860.
\]

Since `delta,kappa>=0`,

\[
U\le U_{max}:={9T_0\over2}-2\pi.
\tag{D76.10}

Together with (D76.3),

\[
\delta\le
\delta_{max}:={U_{max}-C_{76}\over2}+o(1).
\tag{D76.11}

The exact certificate gives

\[
\delta_{max}<0.0873<1.
\tag{D76.12}

Thus the denominator in (D76.7) remains uniformly positive throughout this wider window.

For `U>=C_{76}>2sqrt(Delta_2)`,

\[
m_2'(U)<0,
\qquad
r'(U)=1-m_2'(U)>0.
\tag{D76.13}

Substituting (D76.2) into (D76.6) gives

\[
S=3\pi-5t+2\delta+m_2(U).
\tag{D76.14}

Hence

\[
S_t=-5+{9\over2}m_2'(U)<0,
\]

\[
S_\delta=2-2m_2'(U)>0,
\]

\[
S_\kappa=-{1\over2}m_2'(U)>0.
\tag{D76.15}

These signs are the only monotonicity inputs required by the bootstrap.

---

## 3. Bootstrap map

Suppose a survivor is known to satisfy

\[
z\ge z_j-o(1).
\tag{D76.16}

By (D76.9),

\[
\delta\ge d_j-o(1),
\qquad
d_j:=F(z_j).
\tag{D76.17}

Therefore

\[
U\le U_j+o(1),
\qquad
U_j:=U_{max}-2d_j,
\tag{D76.18}

and because `r` is increasing,

\[
r(U)\le r(U_j)+o(1).
\tag{D76.19}

The signs in (D76.15) show that, under

\[
t\le T_0,
\qquad
\delta\ge d_j,
\qquad
\kappa\ge0,
\]

the shared retained-mass lower bound is minimized at

\[
t=T_0,
\qquad
\delta=d_j,
\qquad
\kappa=0.
\]

Thus

\[
S\ge S_j-o(1),
\]

\[
S_j:=
\pi-U_j-{T_0\over2}+m_2(U_j).
\tag{D76.20}

For the complement upper bound (D76.7), use simultaneously

\[
\delta\le\delta_{max},
\qquad
F(z)\ge d_j,
\qquad
r(U)\le r(U_j).
\]

This gives

\[
p\le P_j+o(1),
\]

\[
P_j:=
{\delta_{max}-d_j+r(U_j)^2
\over2(1-\delta_{max})}.
\tag{D76.21}

Combining with (D76.5),

\[
\boxed{
z\ge S_j-P_j-o(1).}
\tag{D76.22}

This map uses only the symbolic inequalities (D76.3)--(D76.9), not the narrow numerical range of the scalar-floor certificate.

---

## 4. Six exact rounds

The exact rational verifier

`research/verify_target_c_dominant_double_outer_trains_g0076_t1860_2026-09-15.py`

uses Machin bounds for `pi`, integer-square-root enclosures, and alternating-series bounds for `sin`.  It proves the rounded-down implications

\[
0\Longrightarrow z>1.9517-o(1),
\tag{D76.23}

\[
1.9517\Longrightarrow z>2.0576-o(1),
\tag{D76.24}

\[
2.0576\Longrightarrow z>2.0748-o(1),
\tag{D76.25}

\[
2.0748\Longrightarrow z>2.0777-o(1),
\tag{D76.26}

\[
2.0777\Longrightarrow z>2.0782-o(1),
\tag{D76.27}

\[
2.0782\Longrightarrow z>2.0783-o(1).
\tag{D76.28}

The smallest exact margin in these six rounded implications is more than `1.5e-5`.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{z>2.0783-o(1).}
\tag{D76.29}

Equation (D76.9) then gives

\[
\delta\ge F(2.0783)-o(1),
\]

and the exact certificate obtains

\[
F(2.0783)>0.04131095.
\tag{D76.30}

Substituting this into (D76.2), with `t<=1.860` and `kappa>=0`, gives

\[
\boxed{U<2.004193+o(1).}
\tag{D76.31}

---

## 5. Convert retained occurrences to complete double-outer trains

Let `M_D` be the number of complete stopped trains in the frozen dominant windows and let `H_D` count those with two selected outer terminal occurrences.

Every complete stopped train has exactly two terminal face incidences.  A train outside `H_D` contributes at most one selected outer terminal occurrence; a train in `H_D` contributes at most two.  Joint-retention weights are at most one per occurrence.  Therefore

\[
z_DD\le M_D+H_D.
\tag{D76.32}

After taking the source limit and then shrinking the phase windows, the dominant-window train mass is at most the total atomic train mass `A`.  The exposed-hull chain gives

\[
M:=\limsup {M_D\over D}
\le A
\le {T-q/2\over D}
\le U.
\tag{D76.33}

Combining (D76.29), (D76.31), and (D76.32)--(D76.33),

\[
\liminf {H_D\over D}
\ge2.0783-2.004193
>0.0740.
\]

This proves (D76.1).

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The dominant O--O structure is now nonvacuous on a substantial current interval:

```text
current scalar floor:  t >= 1.84332
this theorem:           if t <= 1.860,
                        >0.0740 D trains in one exact pi/3 orbit
                        have both selected terminals on the outer walk.
```

Near the lower end the true bootstrap constant is much larger; at `t=1.850` the same map numerically gives about `0.212D`, but that stronger pointwise constant is not needed here.

The next geometric problem is concrete.  One must place a linear family of same-orbit O--O trains, split among at most three exact projective directions, around the cyclic order of a near-round loaded hull while simultaneously maintaining the near-saturated strict-rhombus crossing graph and the actual diameter-bracing structure.  A useful next theorem should constrain this cyclic endpoint order or show that one phase subfamily forces incompatible crossings, a unit equilateral triangle, or a source-preserving shear.

No Level-I closure is claimed.  C-II remains open.
